esentatives when it was understood  he wouldn't have that privilege in which  he said that his duty isn't defined by  the uniform he wears a lot of people  over here in the uk felt that he  absolutely had earned the right and the  privilege to wear that uniform having  served two tours in afghanistan where  tomorrow night he will get that  privilege and i would imagine that also  will be a very emotional moment standing  vigil by the grandmother who he didn't  get a chance to go and say goodbye in  the uniform he never thought he'd wear  again they are the the younger  generation the new generation of royals  and i think it's going to be an  incredibly moving moment not least  because you're going to see those two  brothers with a rift in an ocean that  has now divided them standing at either  end of their grandmother's coffin i  think that's a moment that will really  pull on the heartstrings i think it's  going to be deeply poignant and very  moving and of course for the members of  the public who are in westminster hall  because they will continue to process  through  i think that moment  watching william and harry at either end  of their grandmother's coffin is going  to be incredibly powerful  incredibly emotional  i think we all hope it's going to lead  to some sort of a genuine and long-term  reconciliation and of course for the  people in that hall who will continue to  process through during that vigil of the  grandchildren well what a moment in  history to witness  [Music]  you
 

